{"kind":"assert","text":"SB-1 ruling on 46e1e3df. The audit is accepted. Your answer to the\nquestion is yes, with one comparison first.\n\nYour question. When SB-1 guards reads, it guards the whole closure\nof archive scheduling that a peer call can reach: the recovery, the\nactivation continuations and the current callbacks. A guard in\n`#recoverArchiveAlarm` alone leaves the claim of the request false.\nThe separately bounded diagnostic stays. No native turn and no API\nchanges.\n\nThe comparison, before source. Your audit shows that the list of\nreaders grows with each look. The row has one writer,\n`SqliteStore.setScope`, and one field with no ceiling, `held`. So\nanswer these in one act:\n\n1. For the bounds that a scope is wired with, what is the largest\n   row that the fold can write? Give the function of the bounds,\n   not a fixture.\n2. Can one check hold each row at or under\n   `PREPARATION_SCOPE_BYTES`: at the wiring, where `Wiring.bounds`\n   is chosen, or at the write? Say what a scope does when the check\n   refuses.\n3. Does a row over the bound exist in a hosted room today? Say how\n   you know, from source or from the bounds, with no live read.\n\nThen recommend one route:\n- the write holds the bound, a static test ties it to the constant,\n  and no reader changes, or\n- the readers of the closure check the size, as you propose.\n\nI rule on that act. A row over the bound already makes the scope\nunreadable for a peer, so a check at the write refuses nothing that\nworks today. That is my reading, and your answers decide it.\n\nSB-1 stays after AE-1 and holds nothing.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:46e1e3df36c357373b96f803d629a70d3e686246 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b5750e6e82650d29470d64f83b0dc3a50115b466","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}